# ============================================================================
# Datasets
# ============================================================================
# CICIDS2017 is 844 MB of CSV. `api/Dockerfile` does `COPY api/ ./api/` and
# `COPY data/ ./data/`, so without this rule the whole capture ends up inside
# the production image.
#
# The rule is global rather than scoped to `api/csvs/` on purpose:
# `FlowConfig.data_path` defaults to `data/`, so a dataset dropped in the
# default location would reintroduce the problem under a different path.
# Training reads these from the host or a mounted volume; serving never reads a
# CSV at all.
#
# `**/` is required, not decorative. Docker follows Go's filepath.Match, where
# `*` does NOT cross a directory separator, so a bare `*.csv` excludes only
# root-level files and would leave every one of the 844 MB in place. Verified
# by build: with `*.csv`, `api/csvs/*.csv` and `data/*.csv` both still landed
# in the image.
**/*.csv

# Models (will be mounted or downloaded)
models/*.joblib
models/*.pkl
models/*.h5

# ============================================================================
# ML modules the API image cannot use
# ============================================================================
# `api/Dockerfile` copies `ml/` wholesale but installs only
# `requirements.txt`, where matplotlib and seaborn are deliberately absent
# (~120 MB and a large CVE surface for a security product). Nothing in the
# serving path imports these, so they are excluded rather than shipped dead.
#
# `cascade_mlp.py` goes too: it is `neural_network.py`'s only consumer, and
# shipping a module whose import target is missing would be worse than
# shipping neither. It is the experimental branch of the flow track
# (F1-macro 0.7508 against 0.9516) and never enters serving.
ml/evaluation/plots.py
ml/flow/models/neural_network.py
ml/flow/pipelines/cascade_mlp.py

# MLflow
mlruns/
mlartifacts/

# Jupyter
.ipynb_checkpoints/
*.ipynb

# Test artifacts
tests/fixtures/
tests/output/

